    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41612-025-01294-4",
    "abstract": "Abstract Soil moisture strongly modulates heat waves and droughts by altering land-atmosphere feedbacks, yet its influence on large-scale circulation remains inadequately quantified. Using large-ensemble simulations with the state-of-the-art climate model EC-Earth 3, we demonstrate that interactive soil moisture has a substantial impact on Northern Hemisphere summer circulation climatology. Two experiments were conducted: a fully interactive simulation and one with prescribed soil moisture states. The results reveal pronounced circulation shifts. Relative to the prescribed case, the interactive experiment drives a poleward displacement of the subtropical jets. It strengthens the polar front jet and enhances land-atmosphere coupling, amplifying wave amplitudes over land by ~24%. Interactive soil moisture raises mean summer surface temperatures by up to +1.5 K and extremes by +3.0 K. These findings demonstrate that soil moisture fluctuations can modify mean atmospheric circulation, with important implications for future summer climate projections.",
    "field": "Land-atmosphere interactions",
    "problem": "The influence of soil moisture's two-way interaction with the atmosphere on large-scale circulation climatology remains inadequately quantified, despite its known importance for local heat waves and droughts.",
    "method": "The study compares two large-ensemble climate model experiments\u2014one with fully interactive soil moisture and another with prescribed soil moisture\u2014to isolate and quantify the influence of land-atmosphere feedbacks on Northern Hemisphere summer atmospheric circulation.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify the influence of interactive soil moisture on Northern Hemisphere summer atmospheric circulation by comparing large-ensemble climate model simulations where soil moisture is either fully interactive or prescribed.",
    "model": [
        "EC-Earth3",
        "SWM",
        "AGCM"
    ],
